{
    "title": "Establishment of Class E Airspace; Buckeye, AZ",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 03, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-03/pdf/2025-21812.pdf",
    "summary": "Two federal agencies issued updates aimed at clarity and safety: the U.S. Nuclear Regulatory Commission set sunset dates and public review for several of its rule sections, and the Federal Aviation Administration created new controlled airspace around Buckeye Municipal Airport in Arizona to support safer flying in poor weather.\n\nFor nuclear rules, the commission added an expiration date of January 8, 2027, to certain \u201cinterpretations\u201d and related sections across multiple parts of its regulations. Before any extension, the commission will ask the public to weigh in on the costs and benefits and can extend these sections for up to five years. Any decision to extend, revise, or remove them will be announced publicly. This approach gives the public a clearer voice and encourages periodic checks on whether these rules are still needed. It could also help license holders by pruning outdated language and improving transparency about what remains in force.\n\nWho benefits? Members of the public gain more say and visibility into how nuclear safety rules evolve, and regulated companies may get clearer, more current requirements. Airports, pilots, and passengers benefit from the airspace change in Buckeye, which supports new arrival and departure paths that help flights operate more reliably when visibility is low. The change is expected to have only minimal economic effects and was cleared for environmental impact.\n\nWho might be negatively affected? Some nuclear licensees and state partners may face uncertainty or added effort as they track which sections are extended or allowed to lapse, and they may need to adjust planning if the commission removes or revises a section after review. Around Buckeye, a small number of general aviation pilots could see new routing or procedural adjustments, and nearby residents may notice subtle shifts in where aircraft fly, even if overall impacts are expected to be minor.\n\nTaken together, these actions reflect a push for regular rule housekeeping and practical safety improvements. The commission is building in scheduled checkups with public input, while the aviation agency is fine-tuning local skies to make sure flights can land and depart more safely when conditions are challenging.",
